    <h1>Six hundred coins under the strait: an interview with Maren Kaldestad</h1>
    <p class="byline">Interview by Jonas Brekke · 21 May 2026</p>

    <p>Dr. Maren Kaldestad is a marine archaeologist at the Kvitholm
    Institute and the excavation lead on the <em>Meridian Rose</em>, a
    seventeenth-century merchant wreck discovered in 2023 during a routine
    sonar survey. We spoke after her team's second field season. The
    transcript has been edited for length.</p>

    <p><strong>Tidewrack:</strong> Start with what you're sure of.</p>

    <p><strong>Kaldestad:</strong> The wreck lies at 41 meters, which is
    deep enough that she was never salvaged and never trawled. The hull
    timbers are oak, and the dendrochronology came back very clean: the
    trees were felled in 1684. Combined with the coin dates, we're
    confident the ship went down in or shortly after 1687.</p>

    <p><strong>Tidewrack:</strong> The coins are what made the headlines.</p>

    <p><strong>Kaldestad:</strong> Over the two seasons we recovered 612
    silver coins, the great majority of them Spanish eight-real pieces.
    For a working merchant vessel that is a remarkable amount of specie,
    and it is why the site is guarded. The cargo manifest, if one ever
    existed, hasn't survived — but the hold also carried salt and copper
    ingots, which we have physically recovered, so those at least are not
    speculation.</p>

    <p><strong>Tidewrack:</strong> And what is speculation?</p>

    <p><strong>Kaldestad:</strong> How she sank. I believe she was lost in
    a storm rather than by grounding — the hull shows no strike damage,
    and she settled upright — but I want to be honest that this is my
    reading of the evidence, not a finding. A colleague I respect reads
    the same evidence and thinks she was overloaded.</p>

    <p><strong>Tidewrack:</strong> What does it take to run a dig like this?</p>

    <p><strong>Kaldestad:</strong> The team is fifteen people at peak
    season. The work is funded through 2027 by a grant of 2.4 million
    euros from the Stray Light Foundation. A third season is planned for
    the summer of 2027, and the finds are contracted to go on public
    display at the maritime museum in Bergen in 2028.</p>
  </article>

